  "image_name": "a2e10e63fb4e06e537baaceaed874d92.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several electrochemical energy-storage and electrocatalytic mechanisms and compares how different electrode structures operate. One large section shows common charge-storage routes: simple ion adsorption at a capacitive surface, surface redox on pseudocapacitive materials, ion insertion into layered solids, and phase-transition or redox reactions in solids, liquids, and gas-involving systems. These sketches emphasize how cations, anions, solvated species, oxidants, and reductants interact with different materials during charging and discharging.\n\nAnother section compares electrode architectures, contrasting a conventional dense electrode with a porous 3D conductive framework and a gas-diffusion electrode. The progression highlights increasing access to pores, catalyst layers, and gas pathways, implying improved transport of reactants and products.\n\nA central graph plots current density versus potential, illustrating oxidation and reduction peaks, charge and discharge voltages, open-circuit voltage, and overpotential. It visually links electrochemical hysteresis to energy losses between charging and discharging.\n\nThe final panel places water splitting and other redox couples on potential scales in acidic and alkaline environments. It marks the oxygen and hydrogen evolution reactions relative to common redox mediators, showing how electrolyte pH shifts reaction potentials and affects feasible electrochemical pathways.",
